  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-17-2-3",
    "page_path": "/explore/mining/metal-industries/aluminum/",
    "name": "Aluminum",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/mining/metal-industries/",
    "entity_type": "category",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s aluminum industry includes primary smelting alongside domestic bauxite and alumina production. Its performance must be assessed across distinct material stages, with particular attention to electricity and the difference between primary metal and fabricated products.",
    "tags": ["Aluminum", "Alumina", "Bauxite", "Smelting", "Electricity", "Recycling"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T11:39:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T11:39:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Aluminum in Iran: Smelting, Alumina and Energy",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s aluminum industry, the distinction between bauxite, alumina and primary metal, and why electricity matters to smelting."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Three materials behind one industry",
        "content": "Iran produces bauxite, alumina and primary aluminum, but these are different materials at different stages of production. Bauxite is an ore; alumina is aluminum oxide obtained through refining; primary aluminum is metal produced by electrolysis. Comparing their tonnages without identifying those stages gives a misleading picture of industrial scale. [s1][s2]\n\nThe US Geological Survey’s Iran review identifies SALCO, Iranian Aluminium Company, Almahdi Aluminium Company and Iran Alumina Company as primary aluminum producers. This establishes a sector with several producing companies rather than a single national smelter. It does not, by itself, establish the origin of every company’s raw materials or its operating conditions at the editorial cutoff. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "production-process",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How ore becomes aluminum",
        "content": "Alumina refining and aluminum smelting are separate industrial operations. Refining uses chemical treatment and heat to obtain alumina from bauxite. Smelting then uses the Hall–Héroult process, in which electricity passes through an electrolyte containing dissolved alumina to produce liquid aluminum. Carbon anodes participate in the process and are consumed. [s2]\n\nThe smelter’s electrolytic cells are commonly organized into potlines. Metal is periodically removed from the cells and taken to a casthouse, where it is formed into shapes suitable for subsequent processing. A casthouse therefore belongs to a different stage from the refinery that produces alumina, even when industrial reporting discusses them within the same corporate group. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "value-chain",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The aluminum material chain",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Bauxite ore\"] -->|\"Chemical refining and heat\"| B[\"Alumina\"]\n  B -->|\"Electrolytic smelting\"| C[\"Primary aluminum\"]\n  C -->|\"Casting and forming\"| D[\"Fabricated materials\"]\n  D -->|\"Manufacture\"| E[\"Products in use\"]\n  E -->|\"Collection and recycling\"| F[\"Recovered aluminum\"]\n  F -->|\"Returns metal to processing\"| D\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram distinguishes ore, oxide, primary metal and fabricated products. It is a general material chain, not a map of verified flows between Iranian companies.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "energy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why electricity is central",
        "content": "Electricity is fundamental to smelting because it enables the electrochemical separation of aluminum from alumina. Reliable power is therefore an operating requirement, not simply an overhead expense. The Australian Aluminium Council’s process explanation describes a continuous current passing through the cells, while the International Aluminium Institute places electricity within a wider system of fuel and heat requirements. [s3][s4]\n\nEnergy demand also extends beyond the potline. Mining and moving bauxite require energy, refining needs heat, anode production consumes fuel, and casting and fabrication require further processing. For Iran, the implication is analytical: assessing the industry requires information about both power supply and the other stages of production. Generic process descriptions cannot establish the actual energy intensity of a particular Iranian smelter. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measurement-and-outlook",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What production totals can tell us",
        "content": "Primary aluminum output measures newly smelted metal. It should remain distinct from recycled metal, fabricated products and installed production capacity. Recycling returns aluminum to use through a different route from producing metal from ore, so an assessment of material efficiency needs evidence about collection and processing as well as primary smelting. [s2]\n\nClimate performance likewise depends on more than the quantity produced. Electricity supply, refining heat and smelting-process emissions all matter. These are priorities in IRENA’s global assessment, rather than verified findings about individual Iranian plants. A defensible account of Iran’s future industry must connect those technical requirements with documented local performance, while keeping announced ambitions separate from measured results. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
